<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">28 May in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 28 May, from 585 BCE to 1998.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/lydia" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lydia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">585 BCE <span class="tih-ago">2,610 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Solar eclipse halts a battle between Lydia and Media</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, day turned to night during a battle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Halys</a> river, and the armies made peace. Astronomers date the eclipse to 28 May 585 BCE, one of the earliest precisely dated events.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">621 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,405 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Li Shimin captures Dou Jiande at the Battle of Hulao</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Tang prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a>, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shichong">Wang Shichong</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, held the Hulao Pass against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a>'s relieving Xia army, routed it and captured Dou. Wang surrendered soon after, and the victory gave the Tang control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hulao">Battle of Hulao</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transition-from-sui-to-tang">Transition from Sui to Tang</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/934">934 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,092 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">King Æthelstan begins his invasion of Scotland</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The English king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a>, first king of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> since 927, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> by land and sea with a large force. No record of any battles fought during the invasion has survived, and Æthelstan returned to England later in the year.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1830">1830 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">196 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Andrew Jackson signs the Indian Removal Act</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The law authorised the president to exchange lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> for Native territory in the East. It led to the forced removals known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trail-of-tears">Trail of Tears</a>, in which thousands died.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1871">1871 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">155 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Paris Commune falls after the 'Bloody Week'</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The last Communard defenders were overcome around the Père Lachaise cemetery. Government troops killed thousands during the week, and the Commune became a lasting symbol for the left.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1918">1918 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">108 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Azerbaijan and Armenia declare independence</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">As the short-lived Transcaucasian federation broke up, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> Democratic Republic and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> proclaimed independence. Both were absorbed by Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1920.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1940">1940 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">86 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">King Leopold III surrenders the Belgian army</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After eighteen days of fighting, Leopold III capitulated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> against his government's wishes. The surrender exposed the Allied flank as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a> evacuation gathered pace.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1998">1998 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">28 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pakistan carries out nuclear tests in Balochistan</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> detonated nuclear devices in the Chagai Hills, responding to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s tests earlier that month. Both countries now stood as declared nuclear powers.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a></div></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 28 May <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-the-fearless.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">John the Fearless</span><span class="tp">1371–1419</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-pitt-the-younger.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">William Pitt the Younger</span><span class="tp">1759–1806</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/edvard-benes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Edvard Beneš</span><span class="tp">1884–1948</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/patrick-white"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/patrick-white.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Patrick White</span><span class="tp">1912–1990</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/edward-seaga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/edward-seaga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Edward Seaga</span><span class="tp">1930–2019</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 28 May <span class="n">3</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/saito-dosan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/saito-dosan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Saito Dosan</span><span class="tp">1494–1556</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-dundas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/henry-dundas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Henry Dundas</span><span class="tp">1742–1811</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/edward-viii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/edward-viii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Edward VIII</span><span class="tp">1894–1972</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
